      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">02</span> The problem</h2>
        <p>
          Each switch is driven by an ESP32. But its decision relied on a "current loop" variable hard-coded in
          each capsule, with a "first come, first served" rule.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>As soon as a capsule was moved by hand, this variable became wrong, and the switch made the wrong call.</li>
          <li>A switch left open could send a capsule back into its starting loop.</li>
          <li>When two capsules arrived close together, the rule created needless waits, or even conflicts.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          On the monitoring side, no page could control the switches, and nothing flagged a disconnected
          device.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">03</span> A new switch logic</h2>
        <table class="case-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Before</th><th scope="col">After</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Decision</th><td>Based on the "current loop", hard-coded in the capsule</td><td class="after">Based only on the destination loop, sent by the capsule</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Priority</th><td>First come, first served</td><td class="after">The capsule leaving its loop goes first</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Idle switch</th><td>Could stay open</td><td class="after">Always closed again</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Sensors</th><td>One infrared sensor per switch</td><td class="after">Two, to follow the capsule to the exit</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          Each switch is assigned to a loop, and decides only from the destination the capsule sends it over
          infrared. No more hard-coded state, so no more errors when a capsule is moved by hand.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a capsule is detected, a FreeRTOS task starts. It asks the capsule to slow down if needed, checks
          whether it can enter the loop, then opens the switch or leaves it closed. The opposite switch is
          notified over the serial link. A second infrared sensor, which I wired on each switch, follows the
          capsule to the exit; two waiting lists separate the capsules that leave from those that stay. The
          switch, moved by a stepper motor, is then closed again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Always closing the idle switch removed one identified cause of derailment and of capsules looping
          back.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">04</span> Real-time monitoring</h2>
        <p>On the existing Flask site, I added:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A sidebar, on every page, that polls each device and turns red the ones disconnected from Wi-Fi.</li>
          <li>A switch control page, to open and close them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          As a pair, we added the manual and automatic modes, locked against each other: each mode blocks access
          to the other's pages, to avoid conflicting orders, and the mode change is broadcast to all capsules. We
          also wrote the recall of capsules to their station, whose message goes through four languages.
        </p>
