      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">05</span> The difficulties</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Infrared links that were sometimes unstable.</li>
          <li>Frequent Wi-Fi disconnections, reduced but not solved for good.</li>
          <li>Build errors caused by the board configuration, solved by reworking the build environments.</li>
          <li>Taking over complex existing code, in C, C++, Python and JavaScript.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">06</span> Results and limits</h2>
        <p>
          By the end of the internship, the model had real-time monitoring with disconnection detection,
          insertion of capsules into an active loop with priority handling, locked manual and automatic modes,
          and the new switch logic. Nothing was measured.
        </p>
        <p>
          For lack of time, some points were left open. Leads left to the next team: make the Wi-Fi more
          reliable, add capsules to test the model under load, install a camera to track the capsules.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">07</span> What I take away</h2>
        <p>
          A distributed embedded system, end to end: microcontrollers talking over infrared, serial and Wi-Fi,
          FreeRTOS tasks, wiring and soldering on the model. I also learned to take over code I had not written,
          and to carry a message across four languages.
        </p>
      </section>

